1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,952
Steel at 850 degrees Celsius holds carbon
in solution.

2
00:00:03,952 --> 00:00:09,735
The crystal structure is face-centered
cubic austenite, and carbon atoms sit in

3
00:00:09,735 --> 00:00:11,931
the spaces between iron atoms.

4
00:00:11,931 --> 00:00:17,184
Plunge that steel into water, and it cools
through 500 degrees in under five seconds.

5
00:00:17,184 --> 00:00:18,995
The carbon has no time to leave.

6
00:00:18,995 --> 00:00:23,184
The iron lattice snaps into a different
shape around it, and the result is

7
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:27,259
martensite — a structure so distorted it
resists every attempt to slide.

8
00:00:27,259 --> 00:00:32,037
That distortion is hardness. But the speed
of the quench is not negotiable.

9
00:00:32,037 --> 00:00:35,413
Cool too slowly, and you get something
else entirely.

10
00:00:35,413 --> 00:00:37,584
How does quenching harden steel?

11
00:00:37,584 --> 00:00:40,274
Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon.

12
00:00:40,274 --> 00:00:46,091
At room temperature, pure iron arranges
itself in a body-centered cubic lattice.

13
00:00:46,091 --> 00:00:51,986
Add carbon and heat the mixture above
roughly 727 degrees Celsius, and the iron

14
00:00:51,986 --> 00:00:55,120
switches to face-centered cubic austenite.

15
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:00,491
The carbon atoms, small enough to fit in
the gaps between iron atoms, dissolve into

16
00:01:00,491 --> 00:01:01,397
the structure.

17
00:01:01,397 --> 00:01:05,811
Austenite is soft and ductile. It is not
the hard phase.

18
00:01:05,811 --> 00:01:08,176
It is the phase you cool FROM.

19
00:01:08,176 --> 00:01:12,572
The eutectoid temperature is the boundary
below which austenite becomes

20
00:01:12,572 --> 00:01:15,605
thermodynamically unstable in plain carbon
steel.

21
00:01:15,605 --> 00:01:19,131
Hold steel above that line, and austenite
persists.

22
00:01:19,131 --> 00:01:24,799
Cool it slowly, and austenite transforms
into ferrite and cementite — a mixture of

23
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:29,915
soft iron and hard iron carbide that forms
visible layers called pearlite.

24
00:01:29,915 --> 00:01:34,970
That transformation takes time because
carbon atoms must diffuse through the

25
00:01:34,970 --> 00:01:39,360
lattice to gather into cementite.
Quenching denies them that time.

26
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:44,325
The diffusion coefficient of carbon in
austenite drops rapidly as temperature

27
00:01:44,325 --> 00:01:49,878
At 700 degrees Celsius, carbon atoms can
move micrometers in seconds.

28
00:01:49,878 --> 00:01:53,419
At 400 degrees, they barely move
nanometers.

29
00:01:53,419 --> 00:01:57,982
The transformation to pearlite requires
carbon to travel distances comparable to

30
00:01:57,982 --> 00:02:02,032
the spacing between cementite layers,
typically hundreds of nanometers.

31
00:02:02,032 --> 00:02:06,051
If the steel cools through the
transformation range in less than a

32
00:02:06,051 --> 00:02:10,496
second, diffusion cannot keep pace. The
austenite must do something else.

33
00:02:10,496 --> 00:02:15,950
Cool austenite faster than about 100
degrees Celsius per second, and the carbon

34
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:19,195
atoms cannot move far enough to form
cementite.

35
00:02:19,195 --> 00:02:23,771
The iron lattice tries to shift back to
body-centered cubic, but the trapped

36
00:02:23,771 --> 00:02:27,384
carbon distorts it into body-centered
tetragonal martensite.

37
00:02:27,384 --> 00:02:33,075
The c-axis of the unit cell stretches. The
lattice is under internal stress.

38
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:38,130
That stress is what stops dislocations
from gliding through the crystal.

39
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:43,465
Dislocations are the defects that let
metals deform. Martensite blocks them.

40
00:02:43,465 --> 00:02:45,080
The result is hardness.

41
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,544
Rockwell C hardness measures resistance to
indentation.

42
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:53,683
Martensite in a high-carbon steel can
reach 65 HRC.

43
00:02:53,683 --> 00:02:58,188
Pearlite, formed by slow cooling, reaches
about 25 HRC.

44
00:02:58,188 --> 00:03:03,021
Ferrite, pure iron with almost no carbon,
sits near 10 HRC.

45
00:03:03,021 --> 00:03:07,741
The difference is not subtle. Martensite
is hard enough to cut other steels.

46
00:03:07,741 --> 00:03:11,847
The transformation from austenite to
martensite is not diffusional.

47
00:03:11,847 --> 00:03:13,624
It is a shear transformation.

48
00:03:13,624 --> 00:03:18,867
The atoms move cooperatively, shifting
positions by less than one atomic spacing.

49
00:03:18,867 --> 00:03:23,277
The transformation happens at nearly the
speed of sound in the material.

50
00:03:23,277 --> 00:03:26,895
X-ray diffraction shows the tetragonal
distortion directly.

51
00:03:26,895 --> 00:03:31,739
The ratio of the c-axis to the a-axis
increases with carbon content, confirming

52
00:03:31,739 --> 00:03:34,376
that carbon is the cause of the
distortion.

53
00:03:34,376 --> 00:03:38,473
Water, oil, brine and air all remove heat
at different rates.

54
00:03:38,473 --> 00:03:43,778
Water at 20 degrees Celsius cools steel
faster than oil at the same temperature

55
00:03:43,778 --> 00:03:48,547
because water has higher thermal
conductivity and higher heat capacity.

56
00:03:48,547 --> 00:03:53,721
Brine, water with dissolved salt, cools
faster still because salt disrupts the

57
00:03:53,721 --> 00:03:58,896
vapor blanket that forms on the steel's
surface. That vapor blanket insulates.

58
00:03:58,896 --> 00:04:01,085
Break it, and heat leaves faster.

59
00:04:01,085 --> 00:04:05,529
When hot steel enters water, a vapor layer
forms immediately.

60
00:04:05,529 --> 00:04:08,589
Heat transfer is slow during film boiling.

61
00:04:08,589 --> 00:04:13,869
As the surface cools below the Leidenfrost
point, the vapor collapses and nucleate

62
00:04:13,869 --> 00:04:14,835
boiling begins.

63
00:04:14,835 --> 00:04:20,402
Cooling accelerates. Finally, below 100
degrees Celsius, convection takes over.

64
00:04:20,402 --> 00:04:24,067
The fastest cooling happens during
nucleate boiling.

65
00:04:24,067 --> 00:04:28,399
Oil quenches avoid the vapor stage but
cool more slowly overall.

66
00:04:28,399 --> 00:04:31,784
The choice of medium is a choice of
cooling curve.

67
00:04:31,784 --> 00:04:32,869
Agitation matters.

68
00:04:32,869 --> 00:04:37,751
Stirring the quench bath or moving the
part through the fluid breaks up the vapor

69
00:04:37,751 --> 00:04:39,981
layer and increases the cooling rate.

70
00:04:39,981 --> 00:04:43,301
Still water cools more slowly than
agitated water.

71
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:45,757
Quench tank design accounts for this.

72
00:04:45,757 --> 00:04:50,860
Polymer quenchants, water-based solutions
with added polymers, allow tuning of the

73
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:53,848
cooling rate by adjusting polymer
concentration.

74
00:04:53,848 --> 00:04:56,835
They sit between water and oil in cooling
speed.

75
00:04:56,835 --> 00:05:01,737
The critical cooling rate is the minimum
speed required to suppress pearlite and

76
00:05:01,737 --> 00:05:02,717
form martensite.

77
00:05:02,717 --> 00:05:08,695
For plain carbon steels, estimates range
from 50 to 200 degrees Celsius per second,

78
00:05:08,695 --> 00:05:10,712
depending on carbon content.

79
00:05:10,712 --> 00:05:15,302
Higher carbon lowers the critical rate
because carbon stabilizes austenite.

80
00:05:15,302 --> 00:05:20,199
Alloy additions like chromium, molybdenum
and manganese also lower it by slowing

81
00:05:20,199 --> 00:05:20,811
diffusion.

82
00:05:20,811 --> 00:05:25,224
Measure the cooling rate at the center of
the part, not the surface.

83
00:05:25,224 --> 00:05:27,301
The surface always cools faster.

84
00:05:27,301 --> 00:05:32,981
The TTT diagram maps what happens when
austenite is held at a fixed temperature.

85
00:05:32,981 --> 00:05:36,315
The nose of the curve shows where pearlite
forms fastest.

86
00:05:36,315 --> 00:05:39,707
Cool fast enough to miss the nose, and you
get martensite.

87
00:05:39,707 --> 00:05:42,725
The diagram is specific to composition.

88
00:05:42,725 --> 00:05:48,917
A 1080 steel, with 0.80 percent carbon,
has a different curve than a 1040 steel.

89
00:05:48,917 --> 00:05:54,014
The diagram does not predict continuous
cooling directly, but CCT diagrams,

90
00:05:54,014 --> 00:05:58,568
continuous cooling transformation
diagrams, do. Both are empirical.

91
00:05:58,568 --> 00:06:02,373
They come from controlled experiments, not
theory alone.

92
00:06:02,373 --> 00:06:07,270
Jominy end-quench tests measure
hardenability, the ease with which a steel

93
00:06:07,270 --> 00:06:08,395
forms martensite.

94
00:06:08,395 --> 00:06:13,509
A heated bar is quenched from one end
only. Hardness is measured along the

95
00:06:13,509 --> 00:06:18,125
The distance over which hardness remains
high indicates hardenability.

96
00:06:18,125 --> 00:06:22,411
High-hardenability steels form martensite
even with slow cooling.

97
00:06:22,411 --> 00:06:25,478
Low-hardenability steels require fast
quenches.

98
00:06:25,478 --> 00:06:29,328
The test is standardized and widely used
to compare alloys.

99
00:06:29,328 --> 00:06:35,787
Martensite hardness increases with carbon
content up to about 0.6 percent by weight.

100
00:06:35,787 --> 00:06:39,097
Beyond that, hardness rises more slowly.

101
00:06:39,097 --> 00:06:44,560
At 0.2 percent carbon, quenched martensite
reaches roughly 40 HRC.

102
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:51,403
At 0.6 percent, it reaches 60 HRC. At 1.0
percent, near 65 HRC.

103
00:06:51,403 --> 00:06:55,848
The carbon distorts the lattice more
severely as its concentration rises.

104
00:06:55,848 --> 00:06:59,440
But above 0.6 percent, retained austenite
begins to appear.

105
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:03,523
Some austenite does not transform even
during a fast quench.

106
00:07:03,523 --> 00:07:07,061
Retained austenite is soft. It dilutes the
hardness.

107
00:07:07,061 --> 00:07:12,989
This is why tool steels, which need
maximum hardness, are often held near 0.6

108
00:07:12,989 --> 00:07:14,683
to 0.8 percent carbon.

109
00:07:14,683 --> 00:07:19,127
Higher carbon gives diminishing returns
and risks retained austenite.

110
00:07:19,127 --> 00:07:23,765
Lower carbon gives martensite that is not
hard enough for cutting tools.

111
00:07:23,765 --> 00:07:28,140
The carbon content is chosen for the
application, and the quench is designed to

112
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:30,853
achieve full transformation at that carbon
level.

113
00:07:30,853 --> 00:07:33,550
As-quenched martensite is hard but
brittle.

114
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:36,309
Internal stresses make it prone to
cracking.

115
00:07:36,309 --> 00:07:41,178
Tempering is a controlled reheating,
usually between 150 and 650 degrees

116
00:07:41,178 --> 00:07:46,181
Celsius, that allows some carbon to
diffuse out of the martensite lattice.

117
00:07:46,181 --> 00:07:52,154
The structure remains martensitic, but the
distortion relaxes. Hardness decreases.

118
00:07:52,154 --> 00:07:55,723
Toughness increases. The trade-off is
deliberate.

119
00:07:55,723 --> 00:08:00,367
Temper at 200 degrees Celsius, and you
retain most of the hardness while

120
00:08:00,367 --> 00:08:01,787
relieving some stress.

121
00:08:01,787 --> 00:08:06,993
Temper at 600 degrees, and you get much
tougher steel at the cost of significant

122
00:08:06,993 --> 00:08:07,904
hardness loss.

123
00:08:07,904 --> 00:08:13,136
The tempering temperature is chosen for
the application. A chisel needs hardness.

124
00:08:13,136 --> 00:08:18,219
A spring needs toughness. Both start with
a quench. Both are tempered differently.

125
00:08:18,219 --> 00:08:21,744
During tempering, carbon precipitates as
fine carbides.

126
00:08:21,744 --> 00:08:26,743
The martensite loses its tetragonal
distortion and becomes body-centered cubic

127
00:08:26,743 --> 00:08:29,371
ferrite with dispersed carbide particles.

128
00:08:29,371 --> 00:08:33,943
The carbides are much smaller than the
cementite layers in pearlite, so the steel

129
00:08:33,943 --> 00:08:37,387
remains harder than slowly cooled steel
even after tempering.

130
00:08:37,387 --> 00:08:42,395
The size and distribution of the carbides
depend on tempering temperature and time.

131
00:08:42,395 --> 00:08:47,576
I wanted one clean cooling-rate number for
'fast enough to harden steel.' It doesn't

132
00:08:47,576 --> 00:08:52,203
exist as a constant — the threshold moves
with the alloy's own composition.

133
00:08:52,203 --> 00:08:56,880
I published the range and said the exact
number has to be measured, not looked up.

134
00:08:56,880 --> 00:09:02,419
Chromium, molybdenum, manganese, nickel
and silicon all slow the diffusion of

135
00:09:02,419 --> 00:09:02,923
carbon.

136
00:09:02,923 --> 00:09:05,289
This lowers the critical cooling rate.

137
00:09:05,289 --> 00:09:10,395
An alloy steel can be oil-quenched or even
air-quenched and still form martensite.

138
00:09:10,395 --> 00:09:12,435
This matters for large parts.

139
00:09:12,435 --> 00:09:18,133
A thick section cannot cool at 100 degrees
per second at its core, even in water.

140
00:09:18,133 --> 00:09:21,591
Plain carbon steel would form pearlite in
the center.

141
00:09:21,591 --> 00:09:24,200
Alloy steel forms martensite throughout.

142
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:29,549
This is why aircraft landing gear and
large forging dies are made from alloy

143
00:09:29,549 --> 00:09:31,200
The section size demands it.

144
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:35,917
A water quench on a large plain carbon
steel part would crack from thermal shock

145
00:09:35,917 --> 00:09:38,216
before it hardened all the way through.

146
00:09:38,216 --> 00:09:39,692
Alloy additions buy time.

147
00:09:39,692 --> 00:09:44,356
They let the core cool slowly enough to
avoid cracking while still cooling fast

148
00:09:44,356 --> 00:09:45,891
enough to form martensite.

149
00:09:45,891 --> 00:09:51,337
The numbers in this script trace to ASM
International's published phase diagrams

150
00:09:51,337 --> 00:09:55,899
and transformation kinetics, NIST's
thermodynamic databases for the

151
00:09:55,899 --> 00:10:00,460
iron-carbon system, and ASTM
International's standards for hardness

152
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:01,277
measurement.

153
00:10:01,277 --> 00:10:05,977
The critical cooling rate is stated as a
range because it depends on composition,

154
00:10:05,977 --> 00:10:08,472
and the literature reflects that
variation.

155
00:10:08,472 --> 00:10:13,959
The Rockwell C values are from controlled
tests on known alloys, not theoretical

156
00:10:13,959 --> 00:10:19,720
predictions. The Jominy test is an ASTM
standard method for measuring

157
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:23,128
The critical cooling rate is not a single
number.

158
00:10:23,128 --> 00:10:28,275
It varies with carbon content, alloy
composition, austenite grain size and

159
00:10:28,275 --> 00:10:29,805
prior thermal history.

160
00:10:29,805 --> 00:10:34,950
The range given here, 50 to 200 degrees
Celsius per second, reflects published

161
00:10:34,950 --> 00:10:38,248
measurements on plain carbon and low-alloy
steels.

162
00:10:38,248 --> 00:10:42,121
Specific compositions require specific
testing.

163
00:10:42,121 --> 00:10:47,395
The TTT and CCT diagrams are empirical
maps, not universal laws.

164
00:10:47,395 --> 00:10:49,901
They must be measured for each alloy.

165
00:10:49,901 --> 00:10:55,048
The mechanism — carbon trapping and
lattice distortion — is well understood.

166
00:10:55,048 --> 00:11:00,105
The exact threshold for a given steel is
not predicted from first principles.

167
00:11:00,105 --> 00:11:01,091
It is measured.

168
00:11:01,091 --> 00:11:04,434
Quenching hardens steel by moving faster
than diffusion.

169
00:11:04,434 --> 00:11:08,136
The carbon that would separate into layers
is frozen in place.

170
00:11:08,136 --> 00:11:13,133
The lattice that would relax into soft
ferrite is locked into strained

171
00:11:13,133 --> 00:11:16,359
Speed is the tool. The distortion is the
result.

172
00:11:16,359 --> 00:11:19,517
And the hardness is the distortion made
useful.
